+++
title: Our view on coaching
+++

Therapy, mentoring, and coaching sit on the same rough scale of people
who help you move forward, but they pull in different directions. A
therapist often helps you understand and process the past. A mentor has
usually done the thing you're trying to do, and passes on what they
learned doing it. A coach does neither by default — a coach asks the
questions that help you find your own answer, and holds you accountable
to the steps you decide on yourself.

In practice we sit a little toward the mentoring side of pure coaching.
Someone who already knows exactly what they want mostly needs the
accountability and the outside perspective — that's coaching in its
purest form. But plenty of people arrive only knowing that something
isn't working, not yet able to name what they actually want instead.
Helping someone get clear on that comes first when it's needed, so the
coaching that follows is pointed at something real instead of a guess.

What actually determines whether an engagement helps has less to do with
methodology or domain than most people assume, and more to do with the
relationship itself. Carl Rogers made essentially this argument in 1957,
proposing that a small set of relational conditions — empathy,
genuineness, and unconditional positive regard — were the necessary and
sufficient conditions for real change, more than any particular
technique (Rogers, C. R., "The Necessary and Sufficient Conditions of
Therapeutic Personality Change," *Journal of Consulting Psychology*,
1957). The research since has largely held up: across the psychotherapy
literature, the strength of the working relationship — assessed as early
as the first few sessions — is one of the most consistent predictors of
outcome there is, repeatedly found to matter as much as or more than
which specific approach was used (see, among others, Horvath & Symonds,
"Relation Between Working Alliance and Outcome in Psychotherapy: A
Meta-Analysis," *Journal of Counseling Psychology*, 1991; and Martin,
Garske & Davis, "Relation of the Therapeutic Alliance With Outcome and
Other Variables: A Meta-Analytic Review," *Journal of Consulting and
Clinical Psychology*, 2000).

That's the real reason behind the introductory conversation described in
our terms. Before we accept payment for any engagement, we ask a
prospective client to speak with their prospective coach first. We're
not screening for a good pitch — we're checking, together, whether this
particular pairing is one where real work can actually happen. Only if
you come away feeling aligned with, and believed in by, that coach will
we accept a paid engagement. If it doesn't feel that way, we'd rather
say so before any money changes hands than after.

None of this requires something to be wrong first. Coaching isn't a last
resort or a sign you couldn't figure it out alone — it's a normal thing
capable people do on purpose, the same way serious athletes train with a
coach as a matter of course, not as an admission of weakness. Michael
Jordan, at the height of being the best basketball player alive, still
sought out coaching for himself — not only the team's coaching staff,
but his own personal coaches, by choice. If someone at that level still
wanted a coach in his corner, "you should be able to do this on your
own" was never really the standard. Anyone who wants to get somewhere
can benefit from having someone in their corner to help them get there —
wanting that isn't a shortfall, it's just wanting to actually arrive.

We back that belief with a real commitment, not just a sentiment: every
coach here carries a small number of pro-bono clients, so that lacking
the money for it isn't what stands between someone and this kind of
growth. That capacity isn't spread evenly across every domain, though —
leadership coaching, for instance, generally serves people already
established enough in their career to be leading something in the first
place, so it isn't part of this pool. Pro-bono capacity is directed
instead toward the domains where financial means is more plausibly the
actual barrier standing between someone and the growth they're after.

Sessions are built around whatever's actually alive for you right now,
not a fixed curriculum. Some people come with a specific goal. Some come
because something feels stuck and they can't yet name what. Both are a
reasonable place to start.
